  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "The austerity measures continue across the restaurant. Nagiyev confronts Kostya the bartender to deliver some bad news. Let's listen closely to their exchange in Russian first, and then walk through each line together.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я просто делал свою работу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Мы экономим на всём.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Мы сэкономили на бармене.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вы не можете меня уволить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ты уволен, балбес.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Балбес, да?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это очень несправедливо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ресторан в трудном положении.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Кто будет наливать напитки?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Это больше не твоя проблема.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хорошо, я уйду сегодня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Вот и отлично.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let us break down this interaction.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я просто делал свою работу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I was just doing my job.' The verb for 'were doing' is in the masculine past tense, marked by that 'l' sound at the end.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Мы экономим на всём.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We are saving on everything.' Notice the word order: 'we' comes first, and 'everything' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Мы сэкономили на бармене.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We saved on the bartender.' Here 'we' sets up the idea, while 'bartender' comes at the end for emphasis.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вы не можете меня уволить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You cannot fire me.' The first content word means 'you', and 'to fire' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ты уволен, балбес.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You are fired, idiot.' The key word here means 'you', which Dmitry combines with 'fired'. Then Kostya replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Балбес, да?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'An idiot, huh?' Here 'blockhead' sets up the idea, while 'blockhead' comes at the end for emphasis.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это очень несправедливо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is very unfair.' Kostya puts 'this' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'very much'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ресторан в трудном положении.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The restaurant is in a difficult situation.' In Russian, 'restaurant' leads into 'position' in a very natural everyday phrasing. And Kostya jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Кто будет наливать напитки?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Who will pour drinks?' The first content word means 'who', and 'drinks' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Это больше не твоя проблема.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is no longer your problem.' Notice the word order: 'this' comes first, and 'problem' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хорошо, я уйду сегодня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Fine, I will leave today.' You'll hear 'good' all the time in the show, here connected with 'today'. Dmitry answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Вот и отлично.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'That is excellent.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'perfect' straight to 'perfect'. Now let us listen to the conversation again without interruption.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
